hey everyone this is kevin from  thechesswebsite.com and today we're  going to be going over my favorite trap  in the ponziani opening panziani starts  out with  e4 e5 knight f3 knight c6  and then pawn two c3 this is the  ponziani preparing for d4  this pawn on c3 really really just  another pawn to support  this central push from white trying to  control that center of the board  knight to f6 pretty common from black  here and then d4 now black has  a lot of options here but we're going to  be specifically looking at  if the knight takes here on e4 this is a  pawn that's unprotected makes a lot of  sense for black to go ahead and take  that it's not really a terrible move  here  but white's just going to keep pushing  up the board here this pawn on e5 it's  only protected by the knight on c6 so  you always want to attack  pieces that are defending other squares  and this e5 square we want to be taking  with our knight here  so attacking this knight and there's not  a lot of great squares for this knight  to go to  if it wants to come back to b8 well now  blacks move  the knight here and then back so really  down  in tempo there so that's not something  you're going to see too often a5  is really a terrible move because after  b4 then the knight's going to fall  there's no squares that it can go to  safely and so really you're going to be  looking at knight to e7 but this  really blocks off a lot of the squares  that black wants to go to get the dark  square bishop involved into the game  get the queen involved into the game and  now white can just take here on e5  and now black really needs to move this  knight because  if they try to play something like d6  which opens up for the light square  bishop  attacking the knight this is going to be  a mistake because bishop two  b5 check pawn two c6 there's capture  here  knight takes here on c6 attacking the  queen queen comes out let's say to b6  but then knight to d4 this is a  discovered check  here from this bishop and this is in a  pretty tough spot you could see either  the king move here to  d8 and then white castles on the king  side  white is up in material black's king is  over here  it can't castle it's going to be  attacked the rest of the game  even if the bishop just comes here to d7  hey just go ahead and exchange right  here  same concept as before white should have  a pretty  good game from here so you're usually  not going to see  the pawn come to d6 because that would  be a mistake many times you'll just see  knight 2 g6 and this is where we're  going to lay  our trap because the knight comes here  to a logical square  if the knight takes it hey it's defended  by both of these pawns this opens up for  the queen and the bishop to get involved  into the game  but now we're going to be playing and  setting our trap and that is  bishop to d3  now from black's standpoint it may look  like white's just trying to  exchange some minor pieces it already is  kind of an awkward opening right away  so they may say yeah you know queen to  e7 and then  if you want to go ahead and take our  knight here on e4 we'll go in exchange  take our knight here or  if we want to take with our our queen  you know they have some options both  sides are equal in material  everything is good so many times if  that's what black thinks they may look  for another option  and so they may find the move which  isn't hard to find here  because anytime a knight on e4 or g4  arrives then many times they're looking  for the move knight takes on f2 and they  may see that okay  they take a pawn here and after the king  captures then  knight takes on e5 black is up upon a  material and this king is completely  exposed black can attack this the rest  of the game  it's going to be very difficult for  white to continue but that's exactly  what we want  black to do take this pawn here on f2  and feel really good about themselves  because we're going to be taking  with our bishop here on g6 now in this  position they should just really take  our bishop i mean they could also  if they wanted to get their bishop  involved maybe bishop here to c5 is a  reasonable move  but they may look at this and say what  is going on here are you just going to  give me  your queen absolutely we're just going  to give you  our queen because after knight takes  here on  d1 again they have fallen for the trap  we can just play bishop takes here on f7  check king comes to e7 we play  bishop to g5 and now it's time to get  more and more material involved into the  action because we are not done with our  attack  quite yet king comes here to d6  and we play knight two c4 and you can  see  all these squares are off limits can't  come  to e7 because of the bishop can't come  to e6 because of the pawn in the bishop  can't come here to e5  because of the knight here can't take  this pawn on  d5 because of the bishop can't come to  c6 because the pawn it has to come here  to  c5 now white can play the move knight to  a3 yes you can go ahead and take the  queen if you want to  you're gonna win the game in this  situation but you can also play knight  to a3  and the reason you can do that is you're  still going to win  the queen like there's no way for them  to protect themselves like they can't  just take  with their queen here on g5 because then  we just play pawn to  b4 checkmate they can't just take that  pawn if they may look and say okay well  pawn to b4 is going to be checkmate  let's just go ahead and take with their  knight here on  a b2 well then we just play bishop to e3  and that's checkmate they could also  look at that pawn to b4 and say okay  well let's try  pawn two a5 and then we just take their  queen here on  d8 we're preparing to just take pawn  here on  c7 to start gobbling up material here  things are not looking  any better as their king is completely  in the center of the board  this knight here on c4 is protected by  this knight  this pawn still protected by the bishop  up here on f7  we can easily start to gobble up  material come here  to b6 that square is defended by our  knights here  there's just no real good squares for  them to come to even if they want to  gobble up some other material let's say  knight takes here on  uh c3 yes we could take that knight but  another  another position where you could say  okay if i don't find another great move  well i can just go ahead and take this  queen here on d8 and you're going to be  completely  fine so that is the flagship trap  what's what i really like about this is  even if they don't take the trap because  sometimes you you try to lay a trap and  your opponent doesn't take and you're  like oh my goodness  i'm in a really terrible spot what's  nice about this is  even if they don't take it you're not in  a terrible spot like they could easily  not take here on f2 and they just take  on e5 and then you take their  knights here on e4 maybe they play  bishop to c5  you castle on the king side you're equal  immaterial  you have nice central pawn here  your bishop's involvement into the game  you've already castled on the king's  side so  this is why this is my favorite trap in  the ponziani because  i i think it will come up more than you  may think and  you're still in a really good position  even if they don't fall  for the trap so hopefully you guys  enjoyed this video  uh hopefully you want to see more trap  videos because i do have some  really good ones i want to share with  you guys so let me know in the comments  below  if you want to see more of these or if  there's other type of videos like  openings i know a lot of people like to  see those as well  but let me know what you guys think  thanks again so much for watching i'll  see you guys  in the next video  you

